@TooTone That was a nice guess, but it's not correct. You fell victim to the symmetry of your $x$ variable, which (when standardized) is identical to its set of negatives. *That* implies the median of $\\hat{\\beta}$ equals $\\beta$. For an illustrative counterexample do this: `x <- c(1:33 / 10^6, 1);
n <- 500;
y <- matrix(exp(rnorm(length(x) * n) - 1/2), nrow=n);
sim <- apply(y, 1, function(z) coef(lm(z ~ x))[2]);
p.hat <- sum(sim > 0) / n;
(p.hat - 1/2) / sqrt(p.hat * (1 - p.hat) / n)`. Generate Normal variates and re-run to compare.

